#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""devlease: lease throwaway Android emulators and iOS simulators to parallel AI coding agents.

Each lease is a fresh device made from a master image you set up once:
  Android: a slot AVD (an APFS clone of the master with its own quick-boot snapshot) started
           with `emulator -read-only` on a fixed port, so all changes are discarded on release.
  iOS:     `xcrun simctl clone <master>`, deleted on release.
The number of devices per platform is capped (RAM is the limit). State lives in ~/.devlease/
(override with DEVLEASE_HOME).

The owner of a lease is the nearest ancestor agent process (claude, codex, gemini, copilot,
opencode, ...), or the git worktree when run from a plain shell. Leases whose owner exited or
whose worktree is gone are freed automatically.

Using devices (agents):
  devlease acquire android|ios [--wait]   lease a device; prints its serial/UDID
  devlease mine [android|ios]             this owner's devices (serial/UDID and MCP device id)
  devlease release [android|ios]          stop and discard this owner's devices
  devlease status                         all slots and who holds them

Setting up (the user, once per platform):
  devlease init android|ios               create a fresh master, open it for manual setup, build slots
  devlease edit android|ios               change the master's data, then rebuild slots
  devlease rebuild android|ios            rebuild slots only (e.g. after `config set android.slots 3`)
  devlease config [get|set KEY [VALUE]]   show or change settings
  devlease doctor                         check prerequisites and setup

Integration:
  devlease hooks install|uninstall|status [AGENT]   guard hooks for claude, codex, gemini, copilot, opencode
  devlease skill install|uninstall|path             agent skill (prefer `npx skills add gohlinka2/devlease`)
  devlease snippet                                  AGENTS.md section to paste into your instructions
  devlease uninstall                                remove devices, hooks, skill, state and the binary
"""
